2.9 Another submission made by the learned counsel was that there is no machinery provision for recovery of ineligible credit taken by an ISD and since there are no provisions, no action can be taken by the department even against the ISD. It was also submitted that the reasoning given for suppression of fact that trading activity was not informed is incorrect and therefore there is no suppression of fact and hence extended period of limitation is not applicable. 3. Learned AR submitted that Shri Arun V. Bavdekar, General Manager, in his statement on 21/01/2011, has stated that, in addition to manufacture, they do trading also and that the cenvat credit passed on by head office includes service tax credit in respect of services used in head office and the same is attributed to both manufactured goods and traded goods and the services are common services. Shri Anil Kumar Singh, Director's statement was recorded on 22/03/2011 wherein he clarified that they are passing on the credit to Roha unit for the services which are not exclusively used for manufactured goods or traded goods. It was also stated by him that they have never informed the department that they are availing credit on common input services through ISD used for manufactured and traded goods. Learned AR further submitted that Rule 2(1) relating to input services has a main part and another inclusive part and inclusive part is not independent of the main part of the definition. For being eligible for input service, it is required that the input service should be used in or in relation to the manufacture of excisable goods. It was also submitted that the input services for which ISD invoices were raised were relating to management, maintenance and repairs, advertisement, business auxiliary service, banking and financial services, company secretary service, courier, clearing and forwarding etc. Learned AR further submitted that from the statement of Shri Anil Kumar Singh, it is very clear that they have passed on the credit which are commonly used in relation to their manufactured and traded goods. Learned AR also submitted that as per Rule 9(6) of the Cenvat Credit Rules, the burden of proof regarding the admissibility of the credit is on the appellant. It was also submitted that ISD only means office of the manufacturer of final products which receives invoices issued under Rule 4A of the Service Tax Rules. Rule 7 further makes it clear that the said Rule permits distribution of credit by an ISD. Onus that credit availed correctly lies with manufacturer and not ISD. It was further submitted that the recovery of the wrongly taken cenvat credit has to be from the manufacturing unit alone as per Rule 14. Learned AR submitted that the appellant when they took the registration as ISD has indicated only Roha unit and their head office. Thus, strictly speaking, there was no need for them to take registration as ISD. As a single unit, they could have availed the credit even in the name of the head office. However, the same was not done in order to camouflage and mislead the department of not knowing that they are doing the trading activity. It was further submitted that there is no concept of self assessment for ISD. Learned AR submitted that Part K of the self assessment memorandum in clause (b), the relevant portion for the input service distributor, is that they have distributed cenvat credit correctly and hence there is no question of assessment by the input service distributor as input service distributor is only a distributor and does not pay any duty. It was also submitted that in the service tax invoices submitted by the appellant, the description of services and reference number/details are finding place and therefore the judgment of the Godfrey Philips is not applicable to their case. It was further submitted that since the cause of action has arisen in Roha unit, the jurisdictional Commissioner has correctly issued the show cause notice. In support of his contention, he cited the following case laws:-

(i)

Lark Chemicals (P.) Ltd. v. Commissioner of Customs 2014 (301) ELT 138 (Tri. - Mum.);

(ii)

K.P. Abdul Majeed v. CCE&CE 1995 (80) ELT 35 (Mad.);

(iii)

Shankeshwar Metal Corpn. v. Commissioner of Customs 2014 (312) ELT 344 (Tri. - Mum.);

(iv)

Venus Enterprises v. Commissioner of Customs 2006 (199) ELT 405 (Mad.).

