ed on the credit which are commonly used in relation to their manufactured and traded goods. Learned AR also submitted that as per Rule 9(6) of the Cenvat Credit Rules, the burden of proof regarding the admissibility of the credit is on the appellant. It was also submitted that ISD only means office of the manufacturer of final products which receives invoices issued under Rule 4A of the Service Tax Rules. Rule 7 further makes it clear that the said Rule permits distribution of credit by an ISD. Onus that credit availed correctly lies with manufacturer and not ISD. It was further submitted that the recovery of the wrongly taken cenvat credit has to be from the manufacturing unit alone as per Rule 14. Learned AR submitted that the appellant when they took the registration as ISD has indicated only Roha unit and their head office. Thus, strictly speaking, there was no need for them to take registration as ISD. As a single unit, they could have availed the credit even in the name of the head office. However, the same was not done in order to camouflage and mislead the department of not knowing that they are doing the trading activity. It was further submitted that there is no concept of self assessment for ISD. Learned AR submitted that Part K of the self assessment memorandum in clause (b), the relevant portion for the input service distributor, is that they have distributed cenvat credit correctly and hence there is no question of assessment by the input service distributor as input service distributor is only a distributor and does not pay any duty. It was also submitted that in the service tax invoices submitted by the appellant, the description of services and reference number/details are finding place and therefore the judgment of the Godfrey Philips is not applicable to their case. It was further submitted that since the cause of action has arisen in Roha unit, the jurisdictional Commissioner has correctly issued the show cause notice. In support of his contenti